= Personas

_Five ways to survive January 1999 — each with a background, the game choices that make it yours, and the mods that fit._

== Before you start: how "game creation" works here

My Winter Car has no character creator — no name, no face, no skill points. You play the game's protagonist, and your identity is what you *decide*. The game gives you real levers, and every persona below pulls them differently:

- *The car plan.* Do you nurse the Sorbet and grind the Rivett in parallel, or buy the Rivett shell the moment the markka allows and live on the build site? The 500 mk purchase is a persona-defining decision.
- *The money path.* Factory shifts, taxi routes, firewood, flea-market flipping — the job mix is your character sheet.
- *The house rules.* What you may buy vs. what you must find, what counts as winning, whether death is reloadable. Difficulty comes from the rules you keep.
- *The mods.* Layer 1 for the first run; Layer 2 (Rivett Revamp enabled) once you have experience. Personas differ in goals and rules, not in the mod list — every persona plays the same two layers.

#info[
  All five personas run on the same installation — they differ in strategy, jobs, house rules and goals, not in the mods available. Start a separate save per persona and keep one backup per save.
]

== 1. The Assembly Mechanic

#mod(
  name: "The Assembly Mechanic",
  tag: "Difficulty: medium · Focus: the build",
  what: "Background. You apprenticed under a village mechanic for seven winters and you believe every car is a story written in bolts. You are not racing anyone — you are restoring the Corris Rivett the way it deserves: VIN plate decoded on day one, every factory option catalogued, every gasket replaced, every torque felt. The classifieds are your reading material and the garage is your office. The ice race can wait until the car is *right*.",
  source: "",
  install: "",
  risk: "",
)

*Game creation choices*

- *Car plan:* Sorbet stays the reliable earner while the Rivett is bought early and built in strict assembly order. No part fitted before its assembly is complete.
- *Money path:* steady Futufon shifts + classifieds ordering; the taxi only when the build budget demands it.
- *House rules:* VIN-correct parts only until the historic restoration is complete; nothing aftermarket that the factory didn't offer — the GT-listed options are the only exceptions; every assembly tested (engine on the stand, brakes before body panels) before moving on.
- *Goal:* a factory-correct Rivett registered with *historic black-and-white plates* — and only then, a tuned variant for the ice track.
- *Mods that shine:* Rivett Revamp (the dash, grilles and bumpers you are restoring finally look right), Enhanced Graphics (the garage at night is the room you live in), Head Lamp, Better FPS, GTA SA Style Minimap (part-hunting routes).

== 2. The Taxi Man

#mod(
  name: "The Taxi Man",
  tag: "Difficulty: medium · Focus: the map and the fare",
  what: "Background. Laid off from the Futufon line, you answered the classified ad and found your calling behind the wheel of a half-million-kilometer diesel Mercedes. You know every house, every pothole, every shortcut in Alivieska — the sun visor map is memorized, not consulted. You drive the night shift for the better fare, and the meter is your meditation. The Rivett is the retirement plan you are building one fare at a time.",
  source: "",
  install: "",
  risk: "",
)

*Game creation choices*

- *Car plan:* buy the Rivett as soon as the taxi job is secured (it unlocks the taxi), then build it slowly in the evenings between shifts.
- *Money path:* the taxi is the whole economy — mode 2 fares, weekend doubles, luggage always loaded, the 90% free-driving allowance honored to the meter.
- *House rules:* every fare completed, no customer refused; the 40% + 3.7 mk/km arithmetic is the score; the taxi's diesel and sandbags are non-negotiable expenses; no save-scumming a bad fare.
- *Goal:* 100 fares, the map memorized cold, and a Rivett that passes inspection with the taxi's earnings — then the rally sprint, driven like a cabbie (smooth, early, safe).
- *Mods that shine:* GTA SA Style Minimap (taxi blips show *active* jobs — the persona's native tool), AI Highbeam Nerf (night shift survival), Head Lamp (picking up fares after dark), Enhanced Graphics, Rivett Revamp.

== 3. The Factory Hand

#mod(
  name: "The Factory Hand",
  tag: "Difficulty: medium · Focus: survival and the wage",
  what: "Background. You are not here to restore a museum piece — you are here to survive January and build a car that runs. The Futufon shift is the spine of your day: punch in, pack chargers, punch out, eat, sleep, repeat. You spend what you earn on exactly two things — parts and warmth. No lottery, no luxury, no debt. The Rivett gets built in the hours the factory leaves you, and every bolt is one bolt closer to a car that owes you nothing.",
  source: "",
  install: "",
  risk: "",
)

*Game creation choices*

- *Car plan:* the Sorbet is maintained religiously (it is the income stream); the Rivett is bought from the first 500 mk surplus and built cheapest-first — whatever makes it start and roll.
- *Money path:* Futufon shifts first; leaflet and firewood jobs on days off; classifieds only for parts the flea market can't supply.
- *House rules:* one day of work, one day of build, every week; needs met before pleasures — warm food, warm garage, sleep; if a part is too expensive this week, it waits — no debt, no shortcuts.
- *Goal:* a running, inspected Rivett and a healthy bank balance — the quiet win of the disciplined earner.
- *Mods that shine:* Better FPS (the long shifts are sim-heavy), GTA SA Style Minimap (commute and errands), Head Lamp, Enhanced Graphics, AI Highbeam Nerf.

== 4. The Ice Racer

#mod(
  name: "The Ice Racer",
  tag: "Difficulty: hard · Focus: the frozen track",
  what: "Background. You grew up on the jokkis circuit — lawnmower engines in clapped-out shells, frozen lakes, and the smell of studded tires on black ice. The Rivett is not a restoration project, it is a *race car waiting for its body*. You bought the cheapest shell you could find, you will bin the VIN in favour of the GT spec, and the tuning notes on the kitchen table are your gospel: cam, carbs, rev limiter, weight over the driven wheels. The inspection is a speed bump, not a standard.",
  source: "",
  install: "",
  risk: "",
)

*Game creation choices*

- *Car plan:* buy the Rivett fast, strip it to the shell, and build for the GT spec: high-performance engine, LSD, quick steering, lowered springs, tachometer cluster, studs for the ice.
- *Money path:* the fastest-paying grind you can stomach — taxi doubles and firewood contracts; parts budget goes to performance, not polish.
- *House rules:* over-revving is a choice, not an accident — you set the rev limiter low and respect it, or you pay for the engine; every race entry must pass inspection first (the ice-track inspections are strict since the January patch); weight, balance and studs are tuned per track, not once.
- *Goal:* win the *ice track race* and the *rally sprint* in a car you built and tuned yourself.
- *Mods that shine:* Rivett Revamp (the GT grille and bumper, the dash you are racing with), Enhanced Graphics (night practice sessions), Better FPS (clean sim at speed), GTA SA Style Minimap, AI Highbeam Nerf.

#warn[
  The Ice Racer is the high-risk persona: tuning a Pinto past its limits is exactly how engines die above ~9100 RPM. The rev limiter is a budget item, not an accessory — this persona respects it, and so should you.
]

== 5. The Hermit of Alivieska

#mod(
  name: "The Hermit of Alivieska",
  tag: "Difficulty: very hard · Focus: the land and the cold",
  what: "Background. You came back to the family property after years away and found the world has moved on without you — good. You live small: firewood cut with the Kekmet, a garden of flea-market bargains, and a bank account you distrust. The Rivett is not a car, it is a *winter project* — it gets built when the day's heat is secured, and not a markka before. You play with the house rules the game cannot enforce: death is permanent, debt is shame, and comfort is a luxury you earn in firewood.",
  source: "",
  install: "",
  risk: "",
)

*Game creation choices*

- *Car plan:* the Sorbet is the only machine you trust; the Rivett is bought last, from surplus, and built at the pace of the seasons.
- *Money path:* firewood contracts, flea-market trading, classifieds arbitrage — the wage jobs are the fallback, not the plan.
- *House rules:* *death is permanent* — no reloads, no cheats, no shortcuts; never spend more than half the bank balance in a day; every purchased part must be justified by a full tank and a warm house first; sleep before midnight, work before noon.
- *Goal:* a historic-plate Rivett and a full winter survived without a single reload — the purest version of the game.
- *Mods that shine:* Head Lamp (the dark is real for this persona), GTA SA Style Minimap (scouting the map's edges), Enhanced Graphics (the frozen world is the point), Better FPS, AI Highbeam Nerf, and Rivett Revamp — when the hermit finally finishes the car, it deserves to look finished.

#warn[
  The Hermit is the hard mode of this list — everything is a resource, including warmth. If a run stops being fun, soften one house rule; the persona is a lens, not a vow.
]

== How to start a persona run

1. Choose your layer: Layer 1 (new player) or Layer 2 (fresh save, experience under your belt — enable Rivett Revamp in the mod menu).
2. Start a new game and spend the first days as the persona's money path dictates.
3. Write your house rules somewhere visible — three lines on paper beats a forgotten promise in the dark.
4. Keep one backup of the persona's save folder (`%AppData%\LocalLow\Amistech\My Winter Car`) per persona.
5. Play the persona's priorities from day one — the build order, the job mix, and the goal are the identity.

